<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Great Horde</h1><p class="st-meta">1466 CE – 1502 CE (approx.) · capital Sarai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last remnant of the Golden Horde on the lower Volga, whose khan Ahmad's retreat from the Ugra River in 1480 ended Mongol overlordship of Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the mid-15th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> had shed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a> and other states. The rump around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuchuk-muhammad">Küchük Muhammad</a> and his sons, became known as the Great Horde, claiming to be the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochid</a> state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its khans still demanded tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde's</a> heritage, allying with Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmat-khan">Akhmed Khan</a>, or Ahmad, was its strongest ruler. Its nomadic economy suffered as trade shifted and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> cities declined.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1480 Ahmad faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III's</a> army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Ugra River</a> and withdrew without battle, ending Muscovy's tribute. He was killed in 1481 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibak-khan">Ibak Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyumen-khanate">Tyumen Khanate</a>. In 1502 the Crimean khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menli-i-giray">Meñli I Giray</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and destroyed the horde.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/great-horde.jpg" alt="Great Horde" width="320" height="261"><figcaption>Image: Ausis; derivative work by Roniius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Horde.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1466 CE – 1502 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sarai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Küchük Muhammad, Ahmad Khan, Shaykh Ahmad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kipchak Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Horde (Ulug Orda), Uly Orda</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1484&amp;focus=great-horde" aria-label="Great Horde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5590 332 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Great Horde lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5910 588L6352 637M5910 588L5808 852M5910 588L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M5808 852h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5910 588h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5910 588h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sarai. <a href="/atlas?year=1484&amp;focus=great-horde">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
